[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Standardized Error Payloads (RFC 7807 Problem Details for HTTP APIs)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Designing consistent and readable error responses is one of the most critical aspects of building robust REST APIs. Without a standard, every endpoint might return a different error structure, making client-side error handling fragile and unpredictable. RFC 7807 introduces 'Problem Details for HTTP APIs', a standard format for conveying machine-readable errors in HTTP response bodies."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem with Ad-Hoc Error Formats"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Historically, developers invented custom JSON structures for errors. One endpoint might return {"error": "Invalid input"}, while another returns {"message": "Validation failed", "code": 400}. This lack of uniformity forces API consumers to write custom parsing logic for every single service they integrate with, slowing down development and increasing maintenance overhead."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding RFC 7807 Structure"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"RFC 7807 defines a 'problem detail' as a JSON object with a specific set of members. Using the media type 'application/problem+json', APIs can communicate errors clearly. The standard fields include type, title, status, detail, and instance."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"type: A URI reference that identifies the problem type. When dereferenced, it should provide human-readable documentation for the error."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"title: A short, human-readable summary of the problem type that should not change from occurrence to occurrence."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"status: The HTTP status code generated by the origin server for this occurrence of the problem."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"detail: A human-readable explanation specific to this occurrence of the problem."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"instance: A URI reference that identifies the specific occurrence of the problem."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Example of an RFC 7807 Error Payload"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"{
  "type": "https://api.example.com/errors/out-of-credit",
  "title": "You do not have enough credit.",
  "status": 403,
  "detail": "Your current balance is 30, but the cost of this item is 50.",
  "instance": "/account/12345/transactions/9876"
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Extending Problem Details"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"One of the greatest strengths of RFC 7807 is extensibility. APIs can include custom members to provide additional context, such as validation errors for specific input fields. This allows clients to highlight form errors directly in the user interface."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"{
  "type": "https://api.example.com/errors/validation-error",
  "title": "Invalid Request Parameters",
  "status": 422,
  "detail": "One or more fields failed validation.",
  "instance": "/users",
  "invalid-params": [
    {
      "name": "email",
      "reason": "Must be a valid email address."
    },
    {
      "name": "password",
      "reason": "Must be at least 8 characters long."
    }
  ]
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Implementation"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always set the Content-Type header to 'application/problem+json' when returning error payloads."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure the HTTP status code in the response header matches the 'status' field in the JSON body."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provide actionable 'detail' messages that help developers debug issues quickly without exposing internal stack traces or sensitive infrastructure details."}]
